{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 10–30 days show a sustained rise (higher highs / higher lows), add a positive level shift to the whole path. In the examples, true prices sit persistently ~+5 to +10 EUR above the model from about day 7 onward.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: the model is closest in the first ~1–3 days, then the negative bias grows with horizon (largest around days ~7–25). Apply an increasing uplift with lead time (e.g., small +1–2 early, ramping to +6–10 mid-horizon).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the forecast follows the direction but reacts too slowly to bullish acceleration. Use a momentum correction: if recent returns are positive and increasing, steepen the forecast slope (raise mid-horizon levels more than near-term).",
    "Over-mean-reversion after pullbacks: after a dip/spike in history, the model pulls back toward ~72–76 too aggressively, while the market remains elevated (~80–84). Reduce mean-reversion strength when the broader trend is up (keep forecasts closer to the recent high regime).",
    "Late-horizon rollover error: in some cases the model predicts a sharp late drop (e.g., falling into high-60s) that does not occur. Impose a floor/soft-landing constraint in bullish regimes (cap downward drift unless recent momentum turns negative)."
  ]
}
